# The split scene: adaptive panes as assertions (DESIGN.md;
# docs/multicolumn-plan.md). The window asks for panes once; WHICH
# presentation materializes is the size class's answer, and this scene
# drives the size class with real resizes and re-asserts on the far
# side. That round trip is the whole point — a transition no code
# specializes gates nothing, which is why resize_window belongs to this
# milestone rather than preceding it.
#
# WHICH WIDTHS THIS SCENE MAY SAMPLE. Each backend now defers the
# one-pane/two-pane decision to its platform's own component, and those
# components disagree about where the line falls: GNOME's documented
# breakpoint collapses below 400sp, Material's standard directive wants
# 840dp before it will show two panes, and TwoPaneView ships its own
# default in between. Between those bounds a width is legitimately
# one pane on one platform and two on another.
#
# This file is compared byte-for-byte on every lane, so it samples only
# the widths where all of them agree: 360 (below every boundary) and
# 900 (above every boundary). check-steps enforces that, because the
# failure it prevents reads as a backend bug rather than a bad width.
expect_entries 0
expect_title "split"
# Wide: regular, and with nothing pushed the stack cannot be showing
# the wrong thing yet. The split arm is what renders.
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
# Push the detail. On a regular window it lands in the TRAILING pane,
# so the base root is still visible — that is what distinguishes this
# from the serial stack, where the push would cover it.
click button#0
expect_entries 1
expect label#0 "list pane"
expect label#last "detail pane"
expect_split "regular/split"
# THE CLAIM THAT MATTERS, and the one the two lines above cannot make:
# both panes are rendered AT ONCE. `expect label#N` reads kaya's model,
# and `expect_split` reads a value the arm stamped about itself — both
# pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing. These read each platform's
# REAL tree, where an element that is not on screen is not present.
expect_ax label#0 "label/list pane"
expect_ax label#last "label/detail pane"
# THE BACK RULE, asserted where it bites. On a regular window both
# panes are on screen, so the back AFFORDANCE has nothing to reveal and
# does not pop; every platform that ships a back gesture already
# refuses here (Compose's canNavigateBack returns false with both panes
# visible, and neither libadwaita nor NavigationSplitView draws a back
# button in an uncollapsed content pane). Popping anyway would leave a
# blank detail pane, which reads as a bug. The app can still pop itself.
back
expect_entries 1
expect_split "regular/split"
# Narrow: the SAME scene, the same stack, one pane. Nothing in the
# guest re-declared anything — the platform re-decided.
#
# 360 rather than a width nearer the boundary: each platform draws its
# own line now (GNOME collapses below 400sp, Material wants 840dp,
# TwoPaneView has its own default), and this scene is compared
# byte-for-byte across all of them. It may only sample widths where
# every platform agrees (see check-steps' band rule).
resize_window 360x600
expect_split "compact/stacked"
expect_entries 1
# Collapsed, the detail COVERS the list, so back means something again
# and the stack pops the way it always did: list-detail is a
# PRESENTATION of the stack, not a second lifecycle.
back
expect_entries 0
expect label#0 "popped detail"
# And back to regular, because a transition that only works in one
# direction is half a feature.
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
